run_eval/run_loop: cross-agent eval parity for Codex and OpenCode (#10)

Adds --harness codex/opencode alongside the existing claude default.
run_single_query is now a thin dispatcher over per-harness adapters;
_run_single_query_claude's own body is byte-for-byte unchanged -- zero
risk to the already-shipped, tested Claude path. Codex has no discrete
skill tool; a skill load shows up as a command_execution event whose
command is literally "cat <path>/<skill>/SKILL.md" (confirmed live).
OpenCode has a discrete tool_use event with tool == "skill" and
state.input.name == the skill's name -- the cleanest signal of the
three (confirmed live). Both adapters write a throwaway, uniquely-named
proxy skill under .agents/skills/ per query, the same trick the Claude
adapter already uses under .claude/commands/.

No Antigravity adapter: confirmed live (investigating #12) that it
doesn't reliably discover project-local .agents/skills/ skills at all --
only its real global skill directory is read. A working adapter would
need to write into that global directory on every eval query, which
this fork won't do silently. Documented as a reasoned limitation, not a
vague "unverified."

Also found and documented a real OpenCode reliability issue while
building the live integration test: opencode run frequently hangs with
zero stdout, confirmed across many attempts, flag combinations, and
even a brand-new uncluttered directory -- not caused by this adapter.
run_eval.py's existing errored/failed/passed design already tolerates
this structurally.

